course: biology
lesson: 374
title: Ukraine's waters: Danube delta pelicans, Black Sea dolphins, sturgeon and gobies
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In Ukraine's south-west a boat slides through channels of tall reeds where the Danube meets the Black Sea. Mud carried by the great river piles up at its mouth, so this delta builds new land into the sea year after year.
explain: Here a line of great white pelicans swims forward in a horseshoe, drives fish into shallow water, then all the heads dip at once. A pelican's pouch scoops up about 10 litres of water, like a bucket, which it drains before swallowing the fish.
explain: Out in the Black Sea live three kinds of dolphin and porpoise, while the deep water below about 150 to 200 metres has almost no oxygen, so almost nothing but bacteria lives there and ancient shipwrecks lie there almost whole.
explain: Ancient sturgeons with bony plates swim up the Danube, and small big-headed gobies cling to rocks with a sucker; gobies even travelled in ships' water tanks to America's Great Lakes.
fact: The Danube Biosphere Reserve protects reed beds, lakes and islands where the Danube meets the Black Sea, and the Ukrainian part of the delta grows into the sea as river mud builds new land.
fact: The delta is home to hundreds of kinds of birds, fish and frogs.
fact: Great white pelicans fish as a team, driving fish into shallow water and dipping their heads at the same moment; the rare Dalmatian pelican, the biggest pelican, also nests there.
fact: A pelican's pouch can hold about 10 litres of water; it drains the water out of the sides of the bill and swallows the fish, and pelicans do not fly about carrying fish in the pouch.
fact: A pelican has four toes on each foot, all joined by webbing, while a duck has three webbed toes and one free toe; pelicans take off by running along the water slapping these big feet.
fact: The Black Sea has three kinds: the bottlenose dolphin, the common dolphin and the harbour porpoise, which has a blunt face, not a beak.
fact: Black Sea dolphins were hunted for oil and meat until hunting was banned, in the Soviet Union in 1966.
fact: After 2022 scientists counted many more dead dolphins than usual on Black Sea beaches; they think noise from warships, sonar (loud sound pulses ships send out to look underwater) and explosions, plus fires and pollution, may be part of the reason, and still argue how much.
fact: Dolphins rely on sound to hunt and to talk, so loud noise can hurt or confuse them.
fact: Below about 150 to 200 metres the Black Sea has almost no oxygen and is full of a gas that smells of rotten eggs, and shipwrecks over 2,000 years old have been found there almost whole because no worms eat the wood.
fact: Sturgeons are ancient fish that have changed little since the age of dinosaurs; the beluga sturgeon can live over 100 years and grow longer than a car, but overfishing for their eggs and meat and dams blocking their rivers have made them rare.
fact: A sturgeon has five rows of bony plates along its body and four whiskers under its snout to feel for food.
fact: Gobies are small bottom fish of the Black and Azov seas whose front belly fins form a sucker; Berdiansk has a monument to the goby, the Goby Breadwinner, that fed people in hard years.
fact: Ships carry water in tanks to keep steady when lightly loaded, and round gobies travelled in these tanks to faraway ports and spread into the Great Lakes of North America, where they eat local fish eggs and push local fish out.
exercise: Why does the Danube delta grow into the sea? | Mud carried by the river piles up and builds new land | The sea is drying up a little more every single year | People pour sand into it | Reeds grow so tall and thick that they push the sea back
exercise: How do great white pelicans catch fish together? | They swim in a horseshoe, drive fish into shallow water and dip together | They dive from high in the sky one by one, like gannets and terns over the sea | They wait on the shore for fish to jump out | They catch fish with their feet
exercise: How much water can a pelican's pouch hold? | About 10 litres, like a bucket | Only about a small teaspoon of water | About one cup, like a mug of tea | About a whole bathtub full of water
exercise: What does a pelican do before swallowing its catch? | Drains the water out of the sides of its bill | Flies home with the fish in its pouch | Spits out the fish and eats the water | Shakes its head until the fish fall asleep
exercise: How are a pelican's feet different from a duck's? | All four of its toes are joined by webbing | It has no webbing at all between its toes | It has only two toes | It has six webbed toes on each of its big feet
exercise: How does a heavy pelican take off from water? | It runs along the water slapping its feet | It jumps straight up out of the water like a frog | It is pushed up by fish | It waits for a big wave to throw it into the air
exercise: Which three kinds of dolphin and porpoise live in the Black Sea? | Bottlenose dolphin, common dolphin and harbour porpoise | Orca, blue whale and narwhal | River dolphin, beluga whale and sperm whale | Pink dolphin, humpback and seal
exercise: How can you tell the harbour porpoise from a dolphin? | It has a blunt face, not a beak | It has two back fins | It has black and white stripes like a zebra | It has fur
exercise: When did the Soviet Union ban hunting dolphins in the Black Sea? | In 1966 | In 1866 | In 2016 | In 1066
exercise: Why might loud noise in the sea be dangerous for dolphins? | They use sound to hunt and talk, so noise can hurt or confuse them | Noise makes the water hot | Dolphins are deaf, so noise has no effect on them at all, even very loud noise | Noise makes the fish grow bigger
exercise: Why does almost nothing live deep in the Black Sea? | There is almost no oxygen below about 150 to 200 metres | The water down there is boiling hot from the volcanoes under the sea | It is frozen solid | Sharks and giant squid have eaten everything there
exercise: Why are ancient shipwrecks found almost whole in the deep Black Sea? | No worms live there to eat the wood | The ships were made of stone | Divers repair them every year | Salt turns the wood into metal
exercise: How long can a beluga sturgeon live? | Over 100 years | About 2 years or so | About 10 years at most | Only one summer
exercise: What does a sturgeon use its four whiskers for? | To feel for food on the bottom | To help it swim much faster upstream | To breathe air up at the surface | To scare away seals and other hungry hunters
exercise: What do a goby's joined front belly fins make? | A little sucker that holds it to rocks | A sail to catch the wind and the waves | A pair of wings | A sharp spike for stabbing other fish
exercise: Why does Berdiansk have a statue of a goby? | The goby fed people in hard years | A goby once saved the mayor from drowning | Gobies helped to build the old harbour | The goby is the biggest fish there
exercise: How did round gobies reach the Great Lakes of America? | In the water tanks of ships | By swimming across the ocean | Carried on the feet of storks | They were always there, from the start
test: Count them: a pelican stands on both feet. How many webbed toes? | 8 | 6 | 4 | 10
test: Count them: a sturgeon has 5 rows of bony plates and 4 whiskers. How many rows and whiskers together? | 9 | 20 | 10 | 8
test: Count them: a boat crew sees one of each Black Sea kind of dolphin and porpoise. How many back fins altogether? | 3 | 6 | 2 | 0
test: In Finding Nemo, Nigel the pelican flies off with fish held in his pouch full of water. What is right about real pelicans? | They drain the water and swallow the fish, not fly about with a full pouch | They carry fish for days to share with friends | They keep the fish alive in the pouch as pets and let them swim around inside it | They carry their eggs in the pouch
test: «it's a pelican, it can just take off like a sparrow» What is right? | A heavy pelican runs along the water slapping its feet to take off | Pelicans jump straight up from the water | Pelicans can only take off from the tops of tall trees by the river | Pelicans cannot fly at all
test: What would happen to the shipwrecks if worms could live deep in the Black Sea? | The wood would be eaten and the ships would fall apart | The ships would float back up to the surface by themselves | The ships would turn to gold | Nothing, because worms never eat wood
test: «dolphins died cos of the war, 100 percent, end of story» What is the careful answer? | Scientists think noise, fires and pollution may be part of it and still argue how much | Scientists are certain it had nothing to do with it | Dolphins only die of old age | Dolphins died because the water got too cold
test: Why might fishing together work better for pelicans than fishing alone? | Together they trap fish in shallow water where none can escape | Alone they cannot open their big heavy bills wide enough to catch a fish | Fish like to swim towards groups | Fishing alone is not allowed
test: «sturgeon been around since dinosaurs, so they'll be fine» Why are they in danger now? | Overfishing for their eggs and meat, and dams blocking their rivers | Dinosaurs still eat them | Their hard bony plates fall off when they get old, and they cannot grow new ones | They have forgotten how to swim
test: «why do ships even carry water in tanks, that's dumb» What is right? | Ships carry water in tanks to keep steady when they are light | Ships carry water to wash all the fish that they catch out at sea | Ships carry water to feed the gobies | Ships carry water so the crew can make tea all day long
test: What would happen if a river stopped carrying mud to its mouth? | The delta would stop building new land into the sea | The delta would grow faster than ever before | The sea around it would slowly turn into fresh water | The river would flow uphill
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the Ukrainian portion of the Danube delta extends further into the sea with each year. | Sediment transported by the river is deposited at its mouth | The level of the Black Sea is falling rapidly | Reeds displace sea water by their growth | Sand is deposited there by human labour
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the feeding of the pelican. | Water is drained from the pouch before the fish are swallowed | Fish are transported for long distances within the water-filled pouch | Fish are caught with the feet | The pouch serves exclusively for the storage of eggs
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the deep waters of the Black Sea below approximately two hundred metres? | They are almost devoid of oxygen, and few organisms besides bacteria inhabit them | They are richer in oxygen than the surface | They are inhabited principally by dolphins, porpoises and the larger sturgeon | They are frozen throughout the year
exam: Ascertain the manner in which the round goby was conveyed from the Black Sea to the Great Lakes of North America. | In water held in the tanks of ships | By migration across the Atlantic Ocean | By deliberate stocking for sport fishing | Upon the feet and feathers of migrating birds
